2026-09-01T13:03Z claude note SCOPE, stated because two of this ticket's clauses name things that do not exist yet. (1) There is no pipeline worker — that is tranche 3 — so Action 3 lands as the machinery a worker calls rather than as a worker: `watchParentEof` (repair a), `WorkerOrphanGuard.checkParent` on the injected clock (repair b), and `WorkerControl` as the handle a deposed P0 kills. The composition is real and tested; the process at the far end of it is not. (2) There are no record tables — the v2 storage schema is still absent everywhere in the fork — so the CAS commit guard is built as the reusable primitive and exercised against the lease row itself. That narrows what is protected in the test; it does not weaken the test of the guard, which is the same statement either way

2026-09-01T13:03Z claude note DESIGN CHOICES worth the author's eye. (1) The commit guard carries NO expiry term, matching §5.2.5's clause verbatim (`WHERE holder = :uuid AND key = :computed_key`). The reasoning is in `lease.ts` and is arguable, so it is stated out loud: SQLite serializes, so a holder nobody has replaced is still the only writer, and an expiry term would discard completed work whenever a garbage-collection pause outran the TTL while buying no safety. A test pins the consequence — an expired-but-unreplaced holder's commit succeeds. (2) `release()` DOES carry the holder term, asymmetrically and deliberately: a shutting-down process already deposed would otherwise blank its successor's claim on the way out. (3) `renewBeforeLongWork` does not rearm the election cadence. §5.2.5 keeps renewal "decoupled from stage progress"; rearming there recouples them, and a conductor running long units back to back would stop checking exactly while it was busiest. (4) `LEASE_MIGRATION_BOUND_MS` is not a fourth constant but the arithmetic R13 asks for — death just after a renewal expires the row at r + TTL, a rival on the cadence takes it by r + TTL + cadence, and the death was strictly after r, so `lease migration < 30 s` holds on every machine without being measured on any

2026-09-01T13:03Z claude note THE SOAK, and what "exactly one conductor" can honestly assert. Three P0s, each with its OWN SQLite connection to one real file in a temp directory — sharing one `Ledger` handle would have tested a program rather than a protocol, since the row is never contended. Cadences are staggered 3 s apart, because if every P0 checked on the same virtual millisecond the array order would decide every election and a broken statement would be hardest to catch. `kill -9` is a member that stops being polled with nothing running on its way out: no release, no cleanup — the case a lockfile cannot survive. Done twice, each time to whoever currently held it, and each time a DIFFERENT survivor took over. The invariant is stated as §5.2.5 allows rather than as one would like: at most one RUNNING P0 believes it is conductor (the spec says in as many words that two can believe it during a handover, which is why the CAS guard exists), plus the row admits exactly one writer whatever anybody believes. Handover asserted against both readings — ≤ TTL + cadence from the last renewal, and strictly under 30 s from the death

2026-09-01T13:03Z claude note POSITIVE CONTROLS, seven run, and one of them corrected the work. Statement-scale, in `conductor-lease.test.ts`: the self disjunct dropped (a live holder that cannot renew — the row flaps every TTL); the guard without `holder = :uuid` (the deposed conductor's write lands, 1 row changed where the shipped guard changes 0); a pid-shaped holder (a recycled identity renews a live lease); an unconditional release (the successor's row blanked). Cluster-scale, in `conductor-election.test.ts`: a statement that steals from a live holder, and one that reads the expiry against the deadline it would WRITE rather than the one stored — both break the exactly-one invariant, taking `maxSimultaneousConductors` above 1. Plus a clock control: with virtual time stopped, fifty poll rounds produce no takeover at all, which is what makes the bound a claim about WHEN rather than about eventually. In `conductor-orphan.test.ts`: a cadence mis-set above the TTL leaves the orphan running — every deposition assertion polls after advancing past the cadence, so all of them would pass unchanged against a server that checked once an hour

2026-09-01T13:03Z claude note THE MUTANT THAT DID NOT BITE, kept in the suite because it is the finding. The early-steal mutant at a one-heartbeat window (10 s) produced NO simultaneous conductors — the invariant stayed green against a statement that is wrong. Cause: the holder's own renewal has already pushed `expires_at` further into the future than the window reaches, so a mutant must exceed TTL − cadence to be visible at all. Two consequences recorded in the test itself. That margin is what TTL = 2 × heartbeat buys, measured rather than assumed. And a mutant that does not bite is not evidence that an assertion is sharp — the first draft of this control would have reported a false all-clear, which is the same shape as the defect the controls exist to prevent

2026-09-01T13:03Z claude note TWO REAL FINDINGS from the red tests, both fixed in the second commit rather than worked around. (1) `Conductor.standDown()` and the election's deposition path both reached the worker kill, racing to name one event: a deliberate shutdown reported itself as `lease lost`, whichever fired first. The callback now carries a `DepositionReason` from the site that knows, and `standDown` no longer repeats the kill. (2) The first draft of the "a P0 that never checks kills nothing" control was constructed wrong — it advanced past the TTL, which is also past the cadence, so the check WAS due and the control asserted a false premise. Rebuilt as the cadence-above-TTL mutant described above, which tests the same claim and can actually come out the other way

2026-09-01T13:03Z claude note R13's DUPLICATE-COMPUTE BOUND LEFT UNTICKED, honestly. The checkbox reads "failover duplicate work bounded per SPEC's honest R13 restatement" — "never committed twice, and duplicate compute bounded by one embed batch plus one in-flight document's re-fetch and re-segmentation per failover". The `never committed twice` half is demonstrated: the deposed conductor's conditional UPDATE affects zero rows and its write body never executes. The compute half CANNOT be demonstrated in this tranche, because there is no embed batch and no document fetch or segmentation to duplicate — those arrive in tranches 3 and 4. What is built is the mechanism the whole bound rests on. The box stays unticked rather than being read down to the half that was achievable
